'''Pinghua'''{{efn|{{zh|t=平話|s=平话|p=Pínghuà|cy=Pìhng Wá}}; sometimes disambiguated as {{zhi|t=廣西平話|s=广西平话}}}} is a pair of [[Sinitic languages]] spoken mainly in parts of [[Guangxi]], with some speakers in [[Hunan]]. Pinghua is a [[trade language]] in some areas of Guangxi, spoken as a second language by speakers of [[Zhuang languages]]. Some speakers are officially classified as [[Zhuang people|Zhuang]], and many are genetically distinct from most other [[Han Chinese]].<ref name="Anth">{{Cite journal |last1=Gan |first1=Rui-Jing |last2=Pan |first2=Shang-Ling |last3=Mustavich |first3=Laura F. |last4=Qin |first4=Zhen-Dong |last5=Cai |first5=Xiao-Yun |last6=Qian |first6=Ji |last7=Liu |first7=Cheng-Wu |last8=Peng |first8=Jun-Hua |last9=Li |first9=Shi-Lin |last10=Xu |first10=Jie-Shun |last11=Jin |first11=Li |display-authors=3 |date=2008 |title=Pinghua Population as an Exception of Han Chinese's Coherent Genetic Structure |journal=Journal of Human Genetics |volume=53 |issue=4 |pages=303–313 |doi=10.1007/s10038-008-0250-x |pmid=18270655 |doi-access=free |first12=Hui |last12=Li |last13=The Genographic Consortium}}</ref> The northern subgroup is centered on [[Guilin]] and the southern subgroup around [[Nanning]]. The Southern dialect has several notable features such as having four distinct [[checked tone]]s, and using various loanwords from the [[Zhuang languages]], such as the final [[Grammatical particle|particle]] ''[[wikt:wei|wei]]'' for imperative sentences.

==History==
Historically, Pinghua is associated with the earliest [[Han Chinese]] migrants who entered [[Guangxi]] via [[Hunan]] in the 1st millennium AD. The name is said to derive from the Pingnan Jun (平南軍, "Pacify [[Southern China|the South]] Army"), a [[Northern Song]]-era army led by [[Di Qing]] in the 11th century.<ref>de Sousa, Hilário. 2020. ''On Pinghua, and Yue: Some historical and linguistic perspectives''. Crossroads: An Interdisciplinary Journal of Asian Interactions 19(2). 257–295.</ref>
==Classification==
Language surveys in Guangxi during the 1950s recorded varieties of Chinese that had been included in the [[Yue Chinese|Yue]] dialect group but were different from those in Guangdong. Pinghua was designated as a separate dialect group from Yue by the [[Chinese Academy of Social Sciences]] in the 1980s<ref name="Hsing 1991">{{Cite book |last=Hsing |first=Fu-I 邢福义 |title=Xiàndài Hànyǔ |date=1991 |publisher=Gaodeng jiaoyu chubanshe |isbn=7-04-002652-X |location=Beijing |language=zh |script-title=zh:现代汉语 |trans-title=Modern Chinese}}</ref>{{Rp|15}} and since then has been treated as a separate dialect in textbooks and surveys.<ref>{{cite book
  | given = Maria | surname = Kurpaska
  | title = Chinese Language(s): A Look Through the Prism of "The Great Dictionary of Modern Chinese Dialects"
  | publisher = [[Walter de Gruyter]] | year = 2010 | isbn = 978-3-11-021914-2
  | pages = 55–56, 76
  }}</ref>  

Since designation as a separate dialect group, Pinghua has been the focus of increased research. In 2008 a report by the Chinese Academy of Social Sciences of research into Chinese varieties noted an increase in research papers and surveys of Pinghua, from 7 before the 1987 publication of the ''[[Language Atlas of China]]'' based on the revised classification, and about 156 between then and 2004.<ref>{{Cite web |title=[cass report by 王宏宇] |url=http://www.cass.net.cn/file/20080415120275.html |url-status= |language=zh-Hans}}{{deadlink|date=September 2023}} April 2008</ref>

In the 1980s the number of speakers was listed as over 2 million;<ref name="Hsing 1991" />{{Page reference|21}} and by 2016 as 7 million.<ref name="ReferenceA">{{Cite book |last=Yu |first=Jin 余瑾 |title=Guǎngxī Pínghuà yánjiū |date=2016 |publisher=Zhongguo shehui kexue chubanshe |isbn=978-7-5161-8896-5 |location=Beijing |pages=24 |language=zh |script-title=zh:广西平话研究}}</ref>

==Dialects==
Pinghua is generally divided into two mutually unintelligible languages:<ref>{{Cite book |last1=Chappell |first1=Hilary |title=The Routledge Encyclopedia of the Chinese Language |last2=Li |first2=Lan |date=2016 |publisher=Routledge |isbn=978-1-317-38249-2 |editor-last=Chan |editor-first=Sin-Wai |location=Oxon |page=624 |chapter=Mandarin and Other Sinitic Languages}}</ref>
* Northern Pinghua ({{zhi|p=Guìběi}} {{lang|zh|桂北平话}}) is spoken in northern Guangxi, around the city of [[Guilin]], in close proximity with [[Southwest Mandarin]] dialects.
**and also in a some places in Hunan, such as [[Tongdao Pinghua|Tongdao]].
**[[Younian dialect]] (ethnically Yao)
* Southern Pinghua ({{zhi|p=Guìnán}} {{lang|zh|桂南平话}}) is spoken in southern Guangxi, around the city of [[Nanning]].  These varieties form a [[dialect continuum]] with Yue varieties spoken in that part of Guangxi (excluding enclaves of [[Cantonese]], such as in [[Nanning]]).<ref>{{cite book
  | given = Hilário | surname = de Sousa
  | chapter = Language contact in Nanning: Nanning Pinghua and Nanning Cantonese | pages = 157–189
  | title = Diversity in Sinitic Languages
  | editor-given = Hilary M. | editor-surname = Chappell
  | publisher = Oxford University Press | year = 2016 | isbn = 978-0-19-872379-0
  }} p. 162.</ref> Yu Jin subdivides this group into three types:<ref>de Sousa (2016), p. 160.</ref>
** Yongjiang, spoken along the [[Yong River (Guangxi)|Yong River]] around Nanning.
** Guandao ({{zhi|c=官道|l=official road}}), spoken to the east of Nanning in [[Laibin]] and the counties of [[Heng County|Heng]] and [[Binyang County|Binyang]], around the road to the [[Southwest Mandarin]]-speaking city of [[Liuzhou]].
** Rongjiang, spoken along the [[Rong River (Guangxi)|Rong River]] to the north of Liuzhou.

The Zheyuan people of [[Funing County, Yunnan|Funing County]], [[Yunnan]] speak a form of Pinghua. They are located in Dongbo and Guichao, and they migrated from [[Nanning]].

==Phonology==
[[Nanning]] Pinghua has a [[voiceless lateral fricative]] {{IPAblink|ɬ}} for [[Middle Chinese]] {{IPA|/s/}} or {{IPA|/z/}}, for example in the numbers {{IPA|/ɬam/}} "three" and {{IPA|/ɬi/}} "four".<ref>{{cite book | surname = Yan | given = Margaret Mian | title = Introduction to Chinese Dialectology | publisher = LINCOM Europa | year = 2006 | isbn = 978-3-89586-629-6 | page = 204 }}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://ai.glossika.com/|title=Learn a language the most natural way - Glossika|website=Ai.glossika.com|access-date=16 January 2019}}</ref> This is unlike Standard Cantonese but like some other Yue varieties such as [[Taishanese]].

===Tones===
Southern Pinghua has six contrasting tones in open syllables, and four in checked syllables,<ref>{{cite book
  | title = Nánníng Pínghuà cídiǎn | script-title = zh:南宁平话词典 | trans-title = Nanning Pinghua Dictionary
  | surname1 = Tan | given1 = Yuanxiong 覃远雄
  | surname2 = Wei | given2 = Shuguan 韦树关
  | surname3 = Bian | given3 = Chenglin 卞成林
  | location = Nanning | publisher = Jiangsu jiaoyu chubanshe | year = 1997 | isbn = 978-7-5343-3119-0
  | page = 6
  }} (part of the ''[[Great Dictionary of Modern Chinese Dialects]]'', edited by [[Li Rong (linguist)|Li Rong]])</ref> as found in neighbouring Yue varieties such as the [[Bobai dialect]].

